{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfeVsV2PxAC49FZrrzTkr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The repaired candidate passed the self-check: it printed `OK` and got a finite ELPD-LOO of −1162.3 on the 100-draw smoke fit. The admission gates the check doesn't cover — novelty, convergence, and the 30-minute limit on the full fit — haven't been tested yet.\n\n**Model:** `motif_stack_person_alternation`, a refinement of `motif_stack`. People score randomness with Griffiths et al.'s four-motif stack automaton, which is the same for everyone. On top of that, each person has their own weight on how much more often one sequence switches between H and T than the other. That weight is drawn from a population distribution. This targets the critique's strongest gap: people differ much more in their preference for alternation than the incumbent produces (q = 0.008).\n\n**What I changed to fix \"too slow to fit\":**\n- **Automaton settings are now fixed.** I held them at `motif_stack`'s own posterior means on these data, read from its cached fit. They were poorly pinned down in that fit anyway (δ = 0.47 ± 0.19, α = 0.18 ± 0.13), and the max-over-paths likelihood was what made sampling slow. The hypothesis states this as part of the claim. One consequence: these means come from experiment-1 data only, so later experiments won't re-estimate them.\n- **Scores are computed once per pair, outside the model.** The automaton score difference and the alternation-rate difference now come from `compute_features` in numpy. What's left inside the model is a vectorised logistic rule.\n- **Parameters:** `beta`, `side_bias`, and a non-centred person effect (`alt_mu`, `alt_sigma`, `alt_z` with 400 slots). The spare slots let the next design predict new participants.\n\n**Speed and caveats:**\n- The smoke fit sampled one chain of 100 tune + 100 draws in 40 s. From that I expect the full admission fit to take around 10 minutes, but that's an estimate, not a measurement.\n- The smoke-fit ELPD looks well ahead of the incumbent's −1380.4, but a short single-chain fit isn't a fair comparison.\n- The check warned that PSIS-LOO is unreliable: 3.9% of trials have Pareto k > 0.5, above the 1% tolerance. The pipeline only exports models with reliable LOO, so if this persists in the full fit, the model can't be exported as the winner even if it ranks first.\n- I spot-checked the precomputed scores on a few sequences and the ordering looks sensible (HHHHHHHH lowest, irregular 8-flip sequences highest), but I didn't compare them value-for-value against the original graph.\n\nAll three files are written in `candidate_5_repair_1/`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3846,"cache_read_input_tokens":63017,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3846,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b20138fc-b8d9-46d5-b6c5-6333b3fa653d","uuid":"fac069ea-b2aa-44c4-bf0f-20b96204a87a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T02:16:11.830Z","request_id":"req_011CfeVsU476N83pYhBnLJE8"}
